<AcademicLayout title="Yoolkyu Park | Research">
  <h1>Research</h1>

 <p>
  My research interests lie in mathematical and statistical methodology for spatially
  structured biomedical data, particularly histopathology images, single-cell multi-omics, and
  related imaging-derived measurements.
</p>

<p>
  I am currently interested in spatial statistics, marked point process methods,
  topological data analysis, and statistical methodology for structured biological data.
  More broadly, I am interested in scientific settings where mathematical structure and
  statistical inference play a central role in the formulation of the problem.
</p>

<h2>Fields of Interest</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Spatial statistics</li>
  <li>Topological data analysis</li>
  <li>Computational pathology and biomedical imaging</li>
  <li>Single-cell multi-omics</li>
  <li>Probability and Markov chains</li>
</ul>

  <h2>Projects</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://lce.biohpc.swmed.edu/sashimi/">SASHIMI</a>. Spatial analysis for
      AI-segmented histopathology images. With Dr. Qiwei Li at UT Dallas, I studied
      spatial and topological summaries of cell–cell interactions in lung and oral cancer
      histopathology. The first-author manuscript is under review.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.06116">Preprint</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Single-cell multi-omics of the brain.</strong> In Dr. Fuhai Li's lab at
      Washington University School of Medicine, I analyze paired scRNA-seq and scATAC-seq
      data across brain regions and drug conditions. My work includes quality control,
      cell-type annotation, differential expression and accessibility analysis, and motif
      analysis for downstream multi-omics model development.
      <a href="/WashU_Multiomics_Yoolkyu_Park_2026.pdf">Poster (PDF)</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Markov chain mixing times and cutoff phenomena.</strong> Senior honors
      thesis with Dr. Zoe Huang in UNC's Department of Statistics and Operations Research,
      beginning with directed reading in August 2026.
    </li>
  </ul>


